MessageExt host-byte failure ordering — 2026-10-18 UTC

- Pinned source at Java commit `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`
  confirms missing born/store hosts throw `NullPointerException`. For short
  caller buffers, Java writes 4-byte IPv4 or 16-byte IPv6 address bytes before
  `putInt(port)` throws `BufferOverflowException`; buffer position and written
  prefix remain observable. Rust now matches the prefix/position effects while
  retaining typed `MissingAddress` and `BufferTooSmall` errors. Null-host
  constructor behavior is also fixture-backed.
- A first direct `exec:java` invocation ran the previously compiled fixture
  class; the focused Rust differential correctly failed because the checked-in
  JSON lacked the new fields, and Maven `verify` then reported fixture drift.
  Recompiled the Java project before regeneration; `fixtures/messages/records.json`
  now has SHA-256
  `6b1a19fef78c1f88f52d7e0851905a6c4a1d6adb98de90fe2028642880a513b3`.
- Passed: focused `message_ext_socket_address_api_matches_java_551`; Common
  all-feature tests; strict all-target/all-feature Common Clippy; `cargo fmt
  --all -- --check`; `git diff --check`; offline Maven `verify` (37 scenarios,
  fixture drift checks, 66 typed-header and 15 typed-body cross-decodes).
  Full-workspace P18-V02 must still be rerun after remaining source work.
